> API and interpretation

The77 example uses base bound3, so the actual retained base is[2]. The first
positive smooth value is9 squared minus77=4; its zero parity gives X=9,Y=2 and
gcd(7,77)=7. Full positive Q values use signed128 arithmetic and modular
products use unsigned128. Hard base/window bounds keep candidate x squared
representable. Histories are actual accepted relations and dependency attempts,
including the successful final relation. A returned n is unresolved, including
on primes; found is independently defined by a nontrivial exact divisor. The
base-bound study may exhaust a budget and is labelled as attempted finite work.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

